7. System Architecture
FitTrack follows a modular RESTful architecture. Client requests pass through the Express server, are validated by JWT authentication middleware, processed by controllers, stored or retrieved from MongoDB, and, where required, sent to the Google Gemini API through a dedicated AI service layer. Authentication, business logic, AI services and database operations are kept in independent modules, which makes the system easier to maintain, extend and debug.
7.1 Components
Client Application: React.js frontend for registration, login, logging workouts and meals, and viewing AI plans and insights through REST APIs.
Express Server: Receives HTTP requests, handles routing and middleware, and returns responses.
Authentication Middleware: Verifies JWT tokens, protects private routes and restricts AI services to authenticated users.
Route Handlers: Define endpoints for authentication, fitness data, AI features and administration.
AI Service Layer: Communicates with the Google Gemini API to generate workout plans, diet plans, insights and tips.
Database Layer: MongoDB with Mongoose ODM stores user data, logs and AI-generated resources.
7.2 MVC Architecture Pattern
Model: Mongoose schemas for MongoDB collections; handle validation and database interaction.
View: As a REST API, the view layer is the structured JSON response consumed by the React frontend.
Controller: Validates input, performs authentication and authorization, talks to models, calls the Gemini service and returns responses.
Separating these concerns improves readability, simplifies testing and allows new features to be added without affecting existing modules.
